### What is wildcard capture?

When a method accepts `List<?>`, it can read elements as Object but cannot add elements (except null). This is because the compiler does not know what ? represents. The wildcard capture pattern solves this by delegating to a private generic method where the compiler captures ? as a named type parameter T.

### Why can you not write to List<?>?

The compiler blocks writes to `List<?>` because it cannot guarantee type safety. The list might be a List<Integer>, a List<String>, or a List<Account>. Adding any specific type could corrupt the list.

```java
package com.bank.demo;

import java.util.List;
import java.util.ArrayList;

public class WildcardWriteRestrictionDemo {
    static void process(List<?> list) {
        // READ: OK, elements come out as Object
        Object first = list.get(0);
        System.out.println("Read: " + first);

        // WRITE: blocked by compiler
        // list.add("test");    // COMPILE ERROR
        // list.add(42);        // COMPILE ERROR
        list.add(null);          // OK: null is always safe

        // Size, isEmpty, contains: all OK
        System.out.println("Size: " + list.size());
    }

    public static void main(String[] args) {
        List<String> accounts = new ArrayList<>(List.of("SAV2001", "CUR3001"));
        process(accounts);

        List<Integer> amounts = new ArrayList<>(List.of(10000, 25000));
        process(amounts);
    }
}
```

```text
Read: SAV2001
Size: 2
Read: 10000
Size: 2
```

### How does the capture helper pattern work?

The pattern involves two methods. The public method accepts `List<?>` and delegates to a private generic helper that accepts `List<T>`. At the call site, the compiler infers T from the actual argument type, capturing the wildcard.

```java
package com.bank.demo;

import java.util.ArrayList;
import java.util.List;

public class CaptureHelperDemo {

    // Public method with wildcard: cannot do set() directly
    public static void swap(List<?> list, int i, int j) {
        // list.set(i, list.get(j)); // COMPILE ERROR: capture#1 of ?
        swapHelper(list, i, j);      // delegate to capture helper
    }

    // Private capture helper: compiler captures ? as T
    private static <T> void swapHelper(List<T> list, int i, int j) {
        T temp = list.get(i);    // read as T: OK
        list.set(i, list.get(j)); // write T: OK
        list.set(j, temp);        // write T: OK
    }

    public static void main(String[] args) {
        List<String> accounts = new ArrayList<>(List.of("SAV2001", "CUR3001", "FD4001"));
        System.out.println("Before swap: " + accounts);

        swap(accounts, 0, 2);
        System.out.println("After swap: " + accounts);
    }
}
```

```text
Before swap: [SAV2001, CUR3001, FD4001]
After swap: [FD4001, CUR3001, SAV2001]
```

### How does the JDK use wildcard capture?

The JDK uses this pattern in several utility methods. Here is a practical example similar to Collections.reverse.

```java
package com.bank.demo;

import java.util.ArrayList;
import java.util.List;

public class ReverseDemoWithCapture {

    public static void reverse(List<?> list) {
        reverseHelper(list);
    }

    private static <T> void reverseHelper(List<T> list) {
        int size = list.size();
        for (int i = 0; i < size / 2; i++) {
            T temp = list.get(i);
            list.set(i, list.get(size - 1 - i));
            list.set(size - 1 - i, temp);
        }
    }

    public static void main(String[] args) {
        List<String> accounts = new ArrayList<>(List.of("SAV2001", "CUR3001", "FD4001"));
        reverse(accounts);
        System.out.println("Reversed: " + accounts);
    }
}
```

```text
Reversed: [FD4001, CUR3001, SAV2001]
```

### What is the difference between List<?> and List<Object>?

| Feature | List<?> | List<Object> |
|---|---|---|
| Accepts List<String>? | Yes | No (invariance) |
| Read elements | As Object | As Object |
| Write elements | Only null | Any Object |
| Use case | Generic utility methods | Truly heterogeneous list |

```java
package com.bank.demo;

import java.util.ArrayList;
import java.util.List;

public class WildcardVsObjectDemo {
    static void wildcardMethod(List<?> list) {
        // list.add("test"); // COMPILE ERROR
    }

    static void objectMethod(List<Object> list) {
        list.add("test"); // OK: List<Object> accepts Object
    }

    public static void main(String[] args) {
        List<String> strings = new ArrayList<>(List.of("SAV2001"));

        wildcardMethod(strings);   // OK: List<?> accepts any List
        // objectMethod(strings);  // COMPILE ERROR: List<String> != List<Object>
    }
}
```

### What are common mistakes?

#### Mistake: Trying to write to List<?> directly

```java
// WRONG: cannot add to List<?>
static void addDefault(List<?> list) {
    list.add("DEFAULT"); // COMPILE ERROR
}
```

```java
// CORRECT: use capture helper
static void addDefault(List<?> list) {
    addDefaultHelper(list);
}
private static <T> void addDefaultHelper(List<T> list) {
    // This still does not work for adding a String to List<T>
    // because T might not be String.
    // The real fix depends on the use case.
}
```

The key insight is that the capture helper does not magically let you add arbitrary types. It lets you perform operations that are inherently type-safe (like swapping two elements from the same list) by giving the wildcard a name.

### What should a senior engineer know?

- **Capture errors in compiler output.** The error message "capture#1 of ?" means the compiler tried to capture the wildcard but could not resolve it to a concrete type for the operation.
- **OpenJDK implementation.** Some JDK methods (like Collections.swap) use raw types internally instead of the capture helper for historical reasons. New code should use the generic helper pattern.
- **Capture with bounded wildcards.** The pattern works with `? extends T` and `? super T` as well. The helper captures the exact bound.
- **Design guideline.** If a public API uses wildcards, keep the implementation in a private generic helper. This gives callers maximum flexibility while maintaining internal type safety.

### What are common interview follow-up questions?

- "Why can you not add to List<?>?" : The compiler does not know the actual type of ?, so it cannot verify that any addition is type-safe.
- "What is the only thing you can add to List<?>?" : null, because null is assignment-compatible with every reference type.
- "What is the capture helper pattern?" : A private generic method that captures the wildcard as a named type parameter, enabling type-safe operations.
- "Does the JDK use this pattern?" : Yes. Collections.swap, Collections.fill, and Collections.copy use capture helpers internally.
